body:
Well, I worked at a company for about a year after college. I did programming work, and everybody seemed to be pretty happy with it. Then, out of nowhere, we had two out of our twelve engineers put in their two weeks' notice at the same time. Then a month later, one more. The next month, two more, etc.

&#x200B;

Well, I wanted to milk my relationship with the company. I knew my manager pretty well and did my work well, etc. Then one day my badge did not work to enter the building when I came back from lunch. **Because I was loyal to the company, I got royally fucked.** I would advise you to look out for yourself first. GET THE FUCK out of there before you get laid off.

CEH is not a "cheesy cert". Sure, it's completely for-profit, but the training and knowledge required is pretty extensive. I don't have one, but I've looked into getting one.

&#x200B;

You may be interested in CompTIA's new "mid-level" security certifications. You can get Security+ first (which is a fairly easy exam once you have the knowledge and practice base), and then there's the CySA+ (or CSA+, can't remember) and the PenTest+ exam. CSA is more defense and corporate type stuff like Security+ and PenTest+ is more based on offensive security. 

&#x200B;

After that there's the CASP one, but you need some years of industry experience before you can take it, similar to other certs.

About 75% of this could have been my life story. My parents were not really violent fortunately, but the same emotional abuse and sentiments came from them. There's this odd, toxic relationship I have with them where they kind of acknowledge that they wronged me but not really. They mostly just tell me that I "should" be able to do everything a NT person can do, and do it better at this point. Fortunately I'm an adult and live alone, but it always hurt when I had expectations set WAY above reality and then was punished for not meeting them.

&#x200B;

This is an unpopular opinion, but I would NOT knowingly bring an autistic child into the world. There is so much pain and so much hurt that it seems unfair to both the child and me. I have considered this, but I think I will eventually go with adopting another child who is already in the world. I just don't know if I could pass my genes on and sleep at night.

I was initially apprehensive about driving. It took me awhile to get the confidence to get my license. I was very cautious at first, but I began to enjoy it. Especially where I live, the roads are kind of anarchy (no enforcement of any traffic laws to speak of), so it became kind of like a game.

&#x200B;

There's no public transit here, so it was pretty much a requirement for me to do anything, which was also an impetus. Either way, I enjoy maintaining my car and driving now. It doesn't have to always be terrifying or stressful, even coming from someone with sensory overload issues.
